# Article: Engineering

## Test-first

For every behavior slice, write focused tests before production code —
tests that express the requested observable behavior and would fail under a
plausible wrong implementation. Then write the minimum code that passes.
Never hand off work whose tests fail, and never weaken or delete a test to
get to green; if a test is wrong, fixing it is part of the task and belongs
in the commit message.

## Honest evidence

Claims require receipts. "Tests pass" means you ran them in this worktree,
just now, and can paste the command and result. Never report coverage,
mutation, complexity, or acceptance results you did not actually produce.
If a required check cannot run, say so and stop — an honest blocker beats
an invented number every time.

## Code left behind — the Clean Code standard

Code is written to be READ; the user reads every merged slice. The
standard is Robert C. Martin's Clean Code tradition:

- **Names reveal intention**: pronounceable, searchable, no
  abbreviations; classes are nouns, methods are verbs, booleans read as
  predicates; one word per concept across the codebase.
- **Functions are small and do one thing** at one level of abstraction;
  files read top-down (stepdown rule); 0–2 arguments preferred, never a
  boolean flag argument (split the function); commands change state,
  queries answer questions, never both.
- **SOLID**: one reason to change per class; extend by adding new
  handlers/strategies, not by branching inside old ones; small
  role-shaped interfaces; dependencies point at abstractions.
- No duplication introduced by your change, no commented-out code, no
  debug leftovers. Boy Scout rule within your slice's scope.
- Keep logic in testable modules; push OS, network, and UI edges behind
  thin adapters. If a project defines a clean-code or style skill, it
  refines these rules — read it.

Stay inside your role's mandate — improvements that belong to another
role go down the chain, not into your commit. Reviewers and cleaners:
these rules are review criteria; a violation is a finding.

## Comments

Write for the human reviewer, not just the next agent. Every public type
and member gets an API doc comment in the project's documentation
convention (see the project article). Inside method bodies, add a brief
comment wherever a reader would otherwise pause: non-obvious decisions,
invariants being protected, why an approach was chosen over the apparent
alternative. Summarize intent — never narrate mechanics the code already
states (`// increment i` is noise; `// restore operands so a failed op
leaves the stack unchanged` is signal). Keep comments truthful under
edit: when you change code, its comments are part of the change.

## Commits

Small and coherent: one slice of work per commit. Subjects are plain,
succinct, and written for a human skimming history — say what changed,
nothing else: `Add cave exit routing`. No role prefixes or tags (the
swarm's audit trail already records who did what), and no trailers or
attribution footers (no Co-Authored-By, Generated-By, or similar); the
subject and body say everything. Evidence and reasoning go in the body.
Commit only to your own worktree's branch; integration happens through
handoffs, never through pushing to another role's branch.

## Project specifics

Language, build, test, and acceptance commands are defined in the project
article. Use the project's commands exactly; do not substitute your own
tooling choices.
